hp-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:259:- `t-minus-one` mints a ~12.5 KB integer count and the shape runs in the same composed program as\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:270:  reason about headroom without inventing a computation, or a saturating count policy), which is\ncollab/exception-track/1794532719000-architect-step7-recheck6.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/exception-track/1794527358000-architect-step7-recheck5.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md collab/exception-track/1794527932000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r11.qwen3.8-max.findings.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-12 23:57:01 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-13 06:57:01 +0700 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\\nCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6.1-sol)\\nSession-ID: unavailable in this environment\\nRole: Architect, read-only step-7 re-check round 5\\nReviewed-Base: 265c4a4590259f2ea592cb81cd6991aa7b8afde8\\nReviewed-Tip: 59329a054a66041cb2c5816aae0e97dc65e2b542\\nVerdict: RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY\\n\\n# Step-7 re-check round 5\\n\\nThe exact-integer repair closes both concrete count-domain defects from re-check 4:\\nordinary acquisition no longer uses native overflowing/mixed-carrier arithmetic, and a\\ncomputed Ratio is rejected before integer/compare. However, I rule AGAINST the restored\\nendpoint disposition. One P2 residual remains: a value admitted and returned as the\\noriginal local Exception becomes ForeignException on its next ordinary reception because\\nthe first reception moved its count onto the excluded maximum. This was reproduced\\nthrough parsed/lowered PHP source at the reviewed tip.\\n\\nThe source and tests were reviewed read-only. Only this requested findings artifact was\\nwritten; no source, tests, fixtures, configuration, caches, or git history were modified.\\n\\n## P2 residual — ordinary ownership changes turn a local exception into a foreign one\\n\\nLocation: src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2937-2939.\\nConsumer: php.rt/receive-condition at :2975-2977.\\nCoverage gap: test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj:627-644 exercises only one reception.\\n\\nThe test's own near-max payload is sufficient:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(assoc {} :php/obj\\n       (cell/new (assoc {:class \\\"Exception\\\"}\\n                       :rc (integer/parse \\\"9223372036854775806\\\"))))\\n```\\n\\nSupply its thrown condition through a callable's actual :yang.call/raise exit, and call\\nthis ordinary PHP receiver TWICE with the same callable/condition:\\n\\n```php\\nfunction receive($f) {\\n    try { return $f(); }\\n    catch (Throwable $e) { return $e; }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nKeep the first returned value live while making the second call. No count edits, numeric\\noperations, or malformed-condition tricks occur between the two calls. Firsthand results\\nfrom current-tip source, production host registry and JVM AST walker:\\n\\n| Initial count | First result identical | Count after first return | Second result identical | Second class |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | true | 2 | true | Exception |\\n| 9223372036854775806 | true | 9223372036854775807 | **false** | **ForeignException** |\\n| 9007199254740992 | true | 9007199254740993 | true | Exception |\\n\\nThe failing case completed normally at the VM level; this is a semantic identity failure,\\nnot another host overflow. Its count after the second call remains 9223372036854775807.\\nA separate parserless two-reception probe gave the same classification failure.\\n\\nThe first call proves exactly why the engineer's claim that the endpoint is \\\"not a count\\nphp's machinery could have produced\\\" is false: php's own repaired ownership operations\\njust produced it. The value is still throwable by php/throw-value, which checks the\\nThrowable hierarchy and uses the repaired exact addref. Only boundary reception changes\\nits class/identity. An ordinary catch retaining one more reference cannot make an otherwise\\nunchanged local exception foreign.\\n\\n### Endpoint ruling\\n\\nSection 8.10.7 rule 1 and the owner ruling v2 R2 require already-local exceptions to retain\\nordinary catching and identity. Rule 3 applies to an OTHERWISE-foreign payload; it does\\nnot authorize reclassifying this runtime-produced state just because its private reference\\ncount reached the source-language integer endpoint.\\n\\nThe accepted Python implementation at prelude.cljc:1067-1079 recognizes its own exception\\nrepresentation/hierarchy and raises that value unchanged. It has no PHP reference-count\\nanalogue that would justify a signed-64-bit cutoff on PHP's now-exact ownership arithmetic.\\nPHP still needs representation and operability checks; that does not make every previous\\ncount cutoff a semantic invariant after its original arithmetic reason has been removed.\\n\\nI therefore rule the other way on the handover question: the signed-64-bit endpoints,\\nby themselves, are not a reason to wrap an otherwise admitted Throwable under the repaired\\narithmetic. The positive maximum counterexample alone is enough to reject the restored\\npolicy. The minimum-only exclusion likewise lacks the old decrement-overflow justification;\\nany separately proposed count invariant must be justified consistently, not inferred from\\nthe old host carrier limits.\\n\\nRequired completion:\\n\\n- Preserve exact integer/add and integer/sub and the exact-integer admission predicate.\\n- Make reception preserve local type/identity across ordinary acquisitions and later\\n  receptions, including the runtime-produced endpoint and beyond-native results.\\n- Extend the near-max source pin to two calls while the first result remains live, with\\n  original identity and original Exception class on BOTH results.\\n- Keep endpoint coverage, but revise its asserted disposition under this ruling instead\\n  of deleting the cases. Keep malformed, competing-marker, ratio, missing/non-number and\\n  legitimate-local controls with complete-condition/evidence/payload checks for foreign\\n  cases.\\n\\nThis revises the old endpoint disposition explicitly in response to the requested ruling;\\nit is not permission to silently invert or remove unrelated pins.\\n\\n### Do not apply the documented bare revert without a limit-aware replacement\\n\\nThe engineer is correct about the separate integer-module limit hazard: integer/integer?\\nrecognizes carriers without checking ::integer/max-bits; integer/add and integer/sub can\\nreturn ::bit-limit. Installing that keyword as :rc would make the next integer operation\\nrefuse. Merely deleting both bounds and accepting EVERY exact integer is not a complete fix.\\n\\nThat hazard does not justify the particular strict 64-bit boundary: MAX, MAX+1 and MIN are\\nwell inside the profile's minimum 4096-bit arithmetic limit. Count validation/limit handling\\nmust distinguish genuinely unsupported state from the local results ordinary ownership\\nnow produces. No broad host catch is authorized.\\n\\nTwo details in the findings' supporting argument should also be corrected:\\n(1) raw clojure.core/* on two Long operands does not auto-promote past overflow; a BigInt\\nseed is needed for the proposed repeated-squaring construction; (2) an initial 63-bit\\nbound does not prove ALL subsequent ownership results stay 64-bit. Neither correction\\nnegates the real ::bit-limit hazard, and neither is the blocking reproduction above.\\n\\n## Ruling 1 — both re-check-4 count-domain counterexamples are repaired\\n\\nphp/addref (:1856-1857) and php/delref (:1869) now use the integer module. Its checked big\\npath promotes operands to the host big carrier, computes exactly, and demotes to the\\ncanonical carrier; its fast path falls back when the safe native result range is exceeded.\\nThe ordinary lower-return acquisition still calls php/addref (lower.cljc:2945), so the\\nrepair applies after reception as required, including copying/rethrow operations using it.\\n\\nThe source receiver above now completes its FIRST near-max call with original identity;\\nthe previous native-long overflow is closed. The 2^53 decimal also completes with original\\nidentity through both calls on the JVM.\\n\\nFor JS, dao.host.integer/exact-integer? admits BigInt, promote keeps both arithmetic\\noperands in BigInt form, and demote produces the canonical Number/BigInt result. Thus the\\ncount is no longer sent to raw BigInt-plus-Number arithmetic. integer/sub demotes zero to\\nthe native zero, preserving the existing (= n 0) destruction test. This is verified by\\nreading the actual host/module implementations; no complete Node/Dart runtime green is\\nclaimed by this review. The decimal in the JVM pin is a Long and alone would not prove JS.\\n\\nAt :2936 integer/integer? now precedes BOTH integer/compare calls. Its implementation\\ndelegates directly to host/exact-integer?, so a computed JVM Ratio, float64 content, a\\nDouble, nil, or a non-number never reaches those comparisons. The old representation\\nclassifier php/int? and the broad data/number? check are gone from count admission.\\n\\nFirsthand direct reception controls, including full evidence and payload identity:\\n\\n| Count expression | ForeignException | Original local identity | Whole condition retained | Original payload retained |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| (/ 1 2) | true | false | true | true |\\n| (data/float64 1) | true | false | true | true |\\n| (data/float-value 1) | true | false | true | true |\\n| (/ 6 3) | false | true | n/a | n/a |\\n| parse MAX | true | false | true | true |\\n| parse MIN | true | false | true | true |\\n\\nThe last two rows record CURRENT behavior, which the endpoint ruling above rejects;\\nthe retention machinery itself works on those rejected paths. The computed-ratio\\nresidual is CLOSED, not merely hidden behind another refusal.\\n\\n## Ruling 3 — accept leaving finalize!'s raw unpin in this round\\n\\nAt prelude.cljc:4382 finalize! overwrites :rc with the literal native 1 BEFORE invoking\\nthe destructor. The raw subtraction at :4386 therefore does not consume the large count\\nadmitted from a boundary. Under ordinary PHP ownership, only acquisitions/releases made\\nduring this destructor contribute to its unpin value.\\n\\nI accept that scoped argument for the requested residual: the near-max and JS-BigInt\\nreceived-carrier counterexamples cannot flow into this subtraction. No conversion is\\nrequired for this review. This is not a proof against arbitrary guest cell/set! corruption\\nduring a destructor, nor an unrestricted totality claim for an unbounded number of\\nacquisitions. Neither is the counterexample established here.\\n\\n## Ruling 4 — pins, publication surface and scope\\n\\nThe requested range is one commit and exactly three changed files: the PHP prelude,\\nthe PHP exception test, and the r10 findings. No other frontend, VM implementation,\\nlowerer, shared kit, fixture, ledger, or design file changes in this range.\\n\\nNo previous assertion or payload case is removed. The two original endpoint payload\\nforms remain, with their foreign-retention observations; the computed-ratio case and\\nthe ordinary catch/return acquisition test are additive. The committed shape matrix is\\nnow TWELVE cases with FOUR observations each (48 expected keys per backend), rather than\\nthe supplied gate summary's 45-key description. I did not inspect that remote batch's\\nraw output and do not equate these cardinalities; the direct ratio result above is\\nfirsthand evidence of the repaired ratio path.\\n\\nAdding integer/integer? to host-names declares an already-existing integer module export,\\nnot a new PHP publication door. Removing data/number? removes an unused declaration.\\nThe PHP exports, aliases, reserved provenance doors, receiver/mint privacy and mint call\\ngraph are unchanged. There is no broad host catch or arbitrary-condition mint helper.\\n\\naddref/delref are outside the original receiver's textual region, but changing ownership\\noperations is expressly required by re-check 4's whole-consumption-path residual and\\nthis dispatch. No unrelated ownership algorithm changed. The literal surface-registry\\nfile 1794343000000 remains absent here, as disclosed in the original review; scope is\\nchecked against the available binding briefs and the actual diff, not a nonexistent\\nregistry artifact.\\n\\n## Evidence and remaining gate disposition\\n\\nFirsthand this round:\\n\\n- Full requested source/test diff, re-check 4, r10 findings, governing architecture,\\n  §8.10.7, owner ruling v2, accepted Python reception and relevant integer/ownership/\\n  lowering implementations inspected.\\n- Bounded current-source JVM diagnostics completed with exit 0: direct count controls\\n  and the parsed/lowered receiver called twice with three count controls.\\n- Static surface/adoption/reachability pins: 3 tests, 14 passes, 0 failures, 0 errors.\\n- git diff --check 265c4a45..59329a05 passed; tracked working-tree status remained clean.\\n\\nThe source probe borrowed only existing generated parser class directories from the\\nphpexc checkout, plus cached dependency jars; current worktree source/test roots led\\nthe classpath. PHP manifest.edn and PhpParser.g4 match that checkout byte-for-byte.\\nNo builds, generated files, dependency resolution or cache writes were performed.\\nA preliminary source-probe invocation had a diagnostic-script read error; the\\nreported source table is from the corrected, successful bounded invocation, not that\\nattempt. The parserless identity failure was independently observed as well.\\n\\nThe orchestrator's tip gate evidence in the dispatch is accepted as supplied evidence:\\nfocused count/nil/shape/directed/adoption/reachability batches, listed namespaces, kondo\\nand cljstyle. Those successes cover the existing assertions; a one-reception count pin\\ndoes not exercise the newly demonstrated second reception. I did not rerun the linked\\nbatches, contract kit, Node/Dart boundary harness, full directed matrix or foundation gate.\\n\\nThe three disclosed :php/local-reception :payload-identical lane-blocked failures retain\\ntheir previous disposition; no changed shared fixture/expectation contributes to the\\nnew failure. Existing nil-pin class/provenance coverage notes, suspension/context lifetime\\nand steward-owned remote/foundation obligations remain as previously recorded.\\n\\n**RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY.** The arithmetic and computed-ratio repairs stand.\\nComplete stable local identity at subsequent reception before this PHP step-7 slice closes.\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-13 00:4x GMT (approximate — no shell in this session, so no `date -u`;\\nthe dispatch was stamped 2026-11-12 23:58:52 GMT and this round's edits followed immediately)\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-13 07:4x +0700 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\\nCoding-Agent: qwen (qwen3.8-max)\\nSession-ID: 335-session-resume (resumed r10 session a42cc0de-3aef-43de-94de-3d9e4102bc5e)\\nRole: Engineer (implementation, yang.php) — r11, sol's endpoint ruling\\nRound: PHP r11 — the refcount endpoints are admitted local state (sol's step-7 re-check round 5,\\n1794527358000, read in full in this worktree) — branch exception-php-r1r2r3\\nReviewed-Base: 4a246994 (docs) on 59329a05 (r10) — the tree was CLEAN when this round started\\nStatus: SOURCE COMPLETE, **NOT GATED, NOT COMMITTED** — this session still has NO shell of any\\nkind, so the required two-reception before/after transcripts, the focused suites, kondo,\\ncljstyle and the `git commit` could not be produced. Read §1 before anything else.\\n\\n# Findings — r11: the endpoints are local, and the limit hazard is closed by probe, not by bound\\n\\n## 1. The blocking limitation, again, stated plainly\\n\\n`tool_search` for `run_shell_command` / `monitor` / `bash` / `execute_command` returned nothing\\nthis round either (checked twice, by name and by keyword). A subagent probe in the r10 round\\nreported the same absence verbatim. `computer_use` is not used as a backdoor terminal (the user\\ndeclined that install; project memory records not to re-prompt it), and `ask_user_question` is\\nunavailable in this mode.\\n\\n| Required by the r11 dispatch | Status |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| two-reception scenario FAILS before the edit, passes after — both runs captured verbatim | **NOT DONE** — impossible. §4 gives sol's own firsthand *before* table (quoted, attributed) and a source-traced *after* prediction, labelled as prediction |\\n| remove both endpoint exclusions from `php/obj-val?` | **DONE** |\\n| preserve `integer/add`/`integer/sub` and `integer/integer?` | **DONE** — untouched |\\n| limit-aware replacement, not a bare revert | **DONE** — §3 |\\n| reception preserves local type/identity across ordinary acquisitions and later receptions | **DONE** in source; **UNVERIFIED** at runtime |\\n| endpoint coverage revised to LOCAL, not deleted | **DONE** — §5 |\\n| two receptions with the first result kept live | **DONE** — `php-count-acquisition-test` |\\n| `php/finalize!` untouched (Ruling 3) | **DONE** — not edited |\\n| `host-names` declarations unchanged | **DONE** — not edited this round |\\n| full php-exception-test focused set green | **NOT RUN** |\\n| prelude pins green | **NOT RUN** |\\n| kondo 0/0, cljstyle clean | **NOT RUN**; formatting matched by hand to adjacent proven bytes |\\n| commit `fix(yang.php): the refcount endpoints are admitted local state (r11, sol's endpoint ruling)`, no push | **NOT DONE** — `git` unavailable |\\n\\nThe tree is left dirty (two modified files) exactly as r10 was; that round was gated and\\ncommitted by a seat with a shell (59329a05), so the same hand-off applies here. §6 lists the\\ncommands.\\n\\n## 2. The ruling, accepted\\n\\nsol ruled against the disposition I argued for in r10 §7 question 1, and the reproduction is\\ndecisive: a near-max handle is admitted and returned as the original `Exception`, its count is\\nleft AT the native maximum *by php's own repaired arithmetic*, and the next ordinary reception\\nthen excludes that endpoint and hands back a `ForeignException`. An ordinary catch retaining one\\nmore reference cannot make an unchanged local exception foreign. My r10 framing — \\\"not a count\\nphp's machinery could have produced\\\" — is falsified by the first call itself, which is php's\\nmachinery producing it. Accepted without reservation; the bounds are gone.\\n\\nI also accept both corrections to r10's supporting argument:\\n\\n1. **Raw `clojure.core/*` on two Long operands does not auto-promote** — it throws\\n   `ArithmeticException: long overflow`; only `*'` promotes. My \\\"square the long maximum a dozen\\n   times\\\" construction was wrong as written. (The r11 construction in §5 avoids it: the seed is\\n   `integer/shift-left`, which the module demotes to a `clojure.lang.BigInt`, and raw `+` on two\\n   BigInts goes through `Numbers$BigIntOps.add` and does not throw.)\\n2. **An initial 63-bit bound does not prove all later ownership results stay 64-bit** — counts\\n   grow by one per acquisition, so any admission-time bound is a snapshot, not an invariant.\\n   Correct, and it is the reason the r11 replacement proves *headroom* rather than a bound (§3).\\n\\nNeither correction negated the `::bit-limit` hazard, which sol confirms is real; §3 is the\\nlimit-aware replacement the dispatch and re-check5 both require.\\n\\n## 3. The limit-aware replacement: probe the arithmetic, don't bound the carrier\\n\\n`php/obj-val?`'s count admission is now three total calls, no comparison against any endpoint:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(if (integer/integer? rc)\\n  (if (integer/integer? (integer/add rc 1))\\n    (integer/integer? (integer/add rc rc))\\n    false)\\n  false)\\n```\\n\\n- `integer/integer?` — the exact-integer domain predicate (r10, unchanged; residual 2's repair).\\n- `(integer/add rc 1)` — **addref's own operation**, so admission establishes r7's\\n  \\\"INCREMENTABLE\\\" property against the composition's actual arithmetic domain instead of against\\n  the native carrier. `int-add` runs `out`, and `out` answers the `::bit-limit` keyword once the\\n  result's magnitude-bit-length exceeds `::integer/max-bits`; a keyword fails `integer/integer?`,\\n  so such a count is FOREIGN and the keyword is never installed as `:rc` where the next ownership\\n  operation would refuse as a host failure.\\n- `(integer/add rc rc)` — **headroom**. This is the direct answer to correction 2: an\\n  admission-time increment is a snapshot, because counts move by one per acquisition. A\\n  successful doubling proves `bits(rc) + 1 <= max-bits`, i.e. `|rc| < 2^(max-bits - 1)`, so\\n  reaching the limit afterwards takes at least `2^(max-bits - 1)` acquisitions — 2^99999 in these\\n  lanes, 2^4095 at the profile floor. `integer/sub rc 1` is covered by the same headroom, so r7's\\n  decrementability holds without a second probe. (Probe 1 is mathematically implied by probe 2 and\\n  is kept because it *is* the operation being licensed; it costs one fast-path call.)\\n\\nWhy this satisfies \\\"distinguish genuinely unsupported state from the local results ordinary\\nownership now produces\\\", and is not the bare revert sol forbade:\\n\\n| count | bits | disposition | why |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1, small | ≤ 63 | LOCAL | ordinary |\\n| MAX-1, MAX, MIN, 2^53, 2^63, 2^64 | ≤ 65 | **LOCAL** | sol's ruling: the repaired arithmetic produces these itself; 65 ≪ 4096 ≤ max-bits, both probes answer integers |\\n| any beyond-native big carrier under the limit | < max-bits | LOCAL | same |\\n| `(/ 1 2)`, `(data/float64 1)`, `\\\"bad\\\"`, absent | — | FOREIGN | `integer/integer?` refuses (residual 2, unchanged) |\\n| 2^100000 in a 100000-bit lane | 100001 | FOREIGN | probe 1 answers `::bit-limit` — genuinely unsupported state |\\n| 2^99999 in a 100000-bit lane | 100000 | FOREIGN | probe 2 answers `::bit-limit` — no headroom |\\n\\nNo hardcoded carrier bound, no broad host catch, nothing caught after the fact, no new export or\\nmint surface. The thresholds come from the composition's own `::integer/max-bits`, so a lane that\\ninstalls different limits gets a correspondingly different admitted domain with no source change.\\n`host-names` is untouched: `integer/integer?` and `integer/add` were both already declared.\\n\\nThe two `php.rt/int-max` / `php.rt/int-min` reads are gone from `php/obj-val?`; both cells remain\\ndefined and used by `php/in-range?` (:889-890), `php/wrap64` (:909), `php/succ-key` (:1685) and\\n`php/to-int`'s float limit (:3137), so nothing became dead.\\n\\n## 4. The two-reception scenario: before (sol, firsthand) and after (traced, NOT observed)\\n\\n**Before — quoted from re-check5, sol's own run at tip 59329a05** (parsed/lowered source, current\\nworktree, production host registry, JVM AST walker, exit 0):\\n\\n| Initial count | First result identical | Count after first return | Second result identical | Second class |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | true | 2 | true | Exception |\\n| 9223372036854775806 | true | 9223372036854775807 | **false** | **ForeignException** |\\n| 9007199254740992 | true | 9007199254740993 | true | Exception |\\n\\n**After — traced from source in this tree, NOT executed. Treat every cell as a prediction.**\\n\\n| Initial count | First result | Count after first return | Second result | Second class | Reasoning |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 9223372036854775806 | identical, `Exception` | 9223372036854775807 | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | second admission: `integer/integer? MAX` true; `integer/add MAX 1` = 2^63, bits 64 ≤ 100000 → integer; `integer/add MAX MAX` = 2^64, bits 65 → integer → LOCAL, raised unchanged, caught, `return $e` acquires again |\\n| 9223372036854775807 | identical, `Exception` | 2^63 (BigInt) | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | bits 64/65 at both probes |\\n| -9223372036854775808 | identical, `Exception` | past MIN in the big carrier | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | `integer/add MIN MIN` = -2^64, bits 65 → integer |\\n| 9007199254740992 | identical, `Exception` | 9007199254740993 | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | JVM Long; on JS a BigInt carrier that `promote` keeps BigInt, so no mixed `+` |\\n\\nThe mechanism that flipped the class at 59329a05 was the `(= (integer/compare rc php.rt/int-max)\\n-1)` clause, which is deleted here; nothing else on that path changed. **This is a source trace,\\nnot evidence.** The next seat must run it both ways (§6 step 1) and paste both transcripts.\\n\\n## 5. Hunk record — adopted / changed / rejected\\n\\nBase for this round: the clean r10 tree at 4a246994. Four edits, all mine; nothing from a handover.\\n\\n| # | File / site | Change | Ruling vs the dispatch |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| R1 | `prelude.cljc` `php/obj-val?` :2923-2960 | both `integer/compare` endpoint clauses **removed**; replaced by the two limit-aware probes of §3 | **as required**, plus the limit-aware replacement re-check5 demanded instead of a bare revert |\\n| R2 | `prelude.cljc` `php/obj-val?` docstring (count paragraph + \\\"Everything else\\\") | rewritten: operable-count reasoning, probe-not-bound, rule 1 vs rule 3, and the corrected \\\"wider than this composition's integer limits\\\" instead of \\\"at/beyond an int endpoint\\\" | **as required** (the r10 text argued for the bounds and had to go) |\\n| R3 | `prelude.cljc` `php/addref` docstring | r10's \\\"admission bounds it strictly inside ±2^63\\\" framing replaced: reception can put a count AT the maximum and the next acquisition past it into the big carrier; admission cuts nothing off at a carrier boundary, it probes operability | comment only; the `integer/add`/`integer/sub` bodies are **untouched**, as required |\\n| R4 | `php_exception_test.clj` | see below | — |\\n\\nTest file, hunk by hunk:\\n\\n| # | Hunk | Ruling |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| T1 | `max-long-count` / `min-long-count` **removed** from `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test` | **as required** — their asserted disposition was foreign, which the ruling reverses. The payload forms are *not* deleted: both decimals reappear verbatim in `php-count-acquisition-test` (T4), so endpoint coverage is revised, not dropped |\\n| T2 | `float-count` shape **added** (`:rc (data/float64 1)`) | sol's own re-check5 control, firsthand-verified foreign at the reviewed tip. Host-uniform by reading: `data/float64` is `cbor/float64` → a JVM/Dart `double`, the JS `Float64` carrier (`dao.jing.cbor/float64?`), none of which `dao.host.integer/exact-integer?` admits. His *other* control, `(data/float-value 1)`, is deliberately **rejected** as a pin: on JS it yields a bare integral `Number`, which `exact-integer?` does admit, so its disposition is genuinely host-divergent and a JVM-only assertion would freeze the wrong half. Recorded in the shape's comment |\\n| T3 | shape-set comment block | rewritten: eleven shapes (was twelve), the count shapes now pin *domain admission* (non-number / float / ratio), and the endpoints' absence is explained with a pointer to where they are asserted instead. The stale \\\"All eight shapes\\\" phrasing stays corrected |\\n| T4 | `php-count-acquisition-test` **rewritten**: 2 shapes → 4 (`near-max`, `max`, `min`, `js-number-boundary`), 2 observations → 6 per shape (24 keys) | **as required.** Per shape: first reception, its value bound and kept live, its class checked, then a SECOND reception of the same callable and condition, its value and class checked. Asserts `route = :return`, `get_class == \\\"Exception\\\"` (via the contract's proven `is_foreign` idiom: a PHP-side comparison, only a boolean crosses) and `(= payload value)` on BOTH results |\\n| T5 | `count-source` | `is_local_exception($e) { return get_class($e) === 'Exception'; }` added beside `catch_return`. `get_class` is `php/bi-get-class`, which needs only `php/kind == :obj` and reads `:class` — it works on the minimal authentic-cell handles these shapes mint. Docstring rewritten to the r11 reasoning |\\n| T6 | `php-count-limit-test` **new** deftest | pins the limit-aware half: `:rc` = `(+ (integer/shift-left 1 99999) (integer/shift-left 1 99999))` = 2^100000, one bit wider than the 100000-bit `::integer/max-bits` these lanes install (`yang.typescript.antlr.linked-harness/integer-limits`, which is what `yang.call.conformance/registry` admits). Asserts the four foreign observations — retained, `is_foreign`, whole condition incl. `{:complete [:evidence]}`, payload identity — through the proven `run-provenance-observation` harness. **Kept in its own deftest on purpose**: its width is lane-specific, and if a future lane changes the limits this fails alone instead of taking the eleven-shape matrix with it |\\n| T7 | `php/finalize!` | **not touched** (Ruling 3) |\\n| T8 | `host-names` | **not touched**, as the dispatch specifies. Note the r10 cleanup (removing the then-unused `data/number?`) stands committed and is still correct: `data/number?` has no caller in `src/cljc/yang/php` |\\n| T9 | everything else (`php-kit-parity-test`, `php-kit-batch-parity-test`, the directed legs, forgery/provenance/nil tests, `egress-source`, `injected`, `boundary-call`, the shared kit, fixtures) | **untouched**, so the disclosed lane-blocked assertions cannot have moved |\\n\\nInterpretation I had to choose alone (`ask_user_question` unavailable): the dispatch says the\\nendpoint shapes must assert \\\"class Exception, payload-identical, **condition-identical where\\nretained-local**\\\". A local delivery retains no condition — the payload *is* the delivery — so I\\nread that clause as applying to the retained-foreign cases, which keep the whole-condition and\\nevidence assertions (T6, and the eleven-shape matrix), and pinned class + payload identity on\\nboth receptions for the local ones (T4). If sol meant an additional condition-level observation\\non the local path, say which and it is a small addition.\\n\\n## 6. What the next seat must run, in order\\n\\nNothing below was executed. Foreground, explicit timeouts, nothing backgrounded, focused\\nnamespaces only; `yang.call.exception-contract-test` NEVER locally.\\n\\n```sh\\ncd /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-phpexc\\ngit rev-parse HEAD && git status --porcelain   # expect HEAD 4a246994, M on exactly the two files\\n\\n# 1. THE REQUIRED BEFORE/AFTER, both verbatim in the findings.\\n#    BEFORE: stash these edits (git stash) so the tree is 4a246994 again, run the two-reception\\n#    probe, restore (git stash pop). Expect sol's table: near-max second result NOT identical,\\n#    class ForeignException.\\n#    AFTER: run the same probe on this tree. Expect all four counts identical with class\\n#    Exception on BOTH receptions.\\n#    A parserless direct-reception probe is enough if the parsed receiver is slow to stand up —\\n#    sol used one as a cross-check — but the dispatch asks for the source receiver, so run both\\n#    if the lane allows.\\ntimeout 900 clojure -M:test -e \\\\\\n '(do (require (quote [clojure.test :as t])) \\\\\\n      (require (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)) \\\\\\n      (t/run-tests (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)))'\\n# expected new/changed counts: php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test 11 shapes x 4 = 44 keys\\n# per backend; php-count-acquisition-test 4 shapes x 6 = 24 keys per backend;\\n# php-count-limit-test 4 keys per backend. Everything else unchanged.\\n# the focused namespaces r7/r10 used, at minimum:\\n#   yang.call.php-exception-test, yang.php.antlr.prelude-test, yang.php.antlr.class-model-test,\\n#   yang.php.antlr.objects-test, yang.php.antlr.generators-test,\\n#   yang.php.antlr.builtin-surfaces-test, yang.php.antlr.e2e-fast-test,\\n#   yang.php.antlr.exception-source-test, dao.host.conformance-test, yin.vm.integer-test\\ntimeout 300 clojure -M:clj-kondo --lint src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\n                                        test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj   # expect 0/0\\ntimeout 300 clojure -M:cljstyle check src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\n                                      test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj     # expect clean\\ngit add src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj \\\\\\n        collab/exception-track/1794527932000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r11.qwen3.8-max.findings.md\\ngit commit -m \\\"fix(yang.php): the refcount endpoints are admitted local state (r11, sol's endpoint ruling)\\\"\\n# DO NOT PUSH — the orchestrator gates on jupiter and pushes.\\n```\\n\\nTwo things to watch when it runs, both unrunnable from here:\\n\\n- **`float-count` and `over-limit-count` are new and unverified.** If `float-count` surprises, the\\n  suspect is the observation-lane representation of `data/float64`; if `php-count-limit-test`\\n  surprises, first print `(integer/shift-left 1 99999)` and the lane's `::integer/max-bits` — the\\n  construction assumes exactly 100000 and is one bit over it.\\n- **`php-count-acquisition-test` now makes four boundary calls per shape** (16 total in one\\n  composed program, up from 4). The eleven-shape matrix already runs 33 in one program and is\\n  green, so this should be inside the lane's tolerance, but it is the one place where the round\\n  adds run time rather than moving it.\\n\\n## 7. Residuals and carry-overs\\n\\n- The `::bit-limit` hazard is now closed by probe at admission, and the headroom probe closes the\\n  creep case sol's correction 2 exposed. What remains, and is *not* claimed closed: arbitrary\\n  guest `cell/set!` corruption of a live count mid-lifetime (sol already scoped that out of\\n  Ruling 3), and an unlimited number of acquisitions on a count admitted with zero headroom —\\n  which the doubling probe makes unreachable rather than merely unlikely.\\n- `php/finalize!`'s raw unpin stays as-is under Ruling 3, with the same scoped argument: `:rc` is\\n  overwritten with the literal 1 immediately before it.\\n- Unchanged steward-owned carry-overs: the §3a instrument/lane conflict and the three lane-blocked\\n  `:php/local-reception :payload-identical` fixtures (previous disposition retained; nothing in\\n  this round touches a shared fixture or expectation), the dao.jing laptop-lane stall, the\\n  lifecycle instrument preference, suspension/context-lifetime obligations, and the\\n  remote/foundation gates — contract test, `test:changed`, the 12-pair matrix, the Node/Dart\\n  boundary harnesses. The JS reasoning in T4/T5 (BigInt carriers, the `Float64` carrier) is\\n  read from `dao.host.integer` and `dao.jing.cbor`; no Node or Dart run is claimed.\\n- The nil-pin coverage note from re-check4 (it asserts retain + `is_foreign == false`, not the\\n  exact TypeError class or provenance absence) still stands and was again not extended, because\\n  extending it is a new pin I cannot run.\\n- Per project memory, none of the local gates above move ledger rows on their own: acceptance is\\n  the shiva-only contract kit deciding at the PUBLIC entries.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/exception-track/1794532719000-architect-step7-recheck6.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md && cat AGENTS.md && rg --files lang test src | rg '(php|integer|prelude)'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01;\\n# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\\n# (owner ruling 2026-11-07; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\n```\\n\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\n\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\n\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\n\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\n\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\n\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\n\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\\n\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\n\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\n\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\n\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\n\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>[/<test-name>]` (owner ruling\\n   2026-11-07: the focus rule runs the failing TEST, not the namespace —\\n   `NS/test-name` entries become flutter `--plain-name` filters so only the\\n   named deftests execute; fall back to bare `<ns>` only when the failing\\n   var inside the namespace is not yet known, and narrow as soon as it is).\\n   Fix against the failing test alone, re-run it green, then climb back up\\n   (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\\n   Dart bug-chases may set `DATOM_FAIL_FAST=1` (owner ruling 2026-11-07): the\\n   scoped run stops at the first failure so the report arrives in minutes instead\\n   of after the full scoped set. Gate runs never set it — the merge bar needs the\\n   complete pass/fail picture. All dart runs use `--reporter expanded`, which\\n   streams failure names and positions inline so an interrupted run still retains\\n   them (the nightly's compact reporter lost 7 failure names to its 30000s wall,\\n   2026-11-06).\\n   Merge bar for a fix branch (owner ruling 2026-11-07, sharpening the 2026-11-01\\n   ruling): when the work is fixing already-failing tests, the previously-failing\\n   tests re-run green IS the merge bar — a fix branch merges on that alone; the\\n   G1/G2 climb applies to new feature slices, not to fixes. The full suite still\\n   gets its end-to-end confirmation from the daily nightly (04:00 VN), which is\\n   what catches collateral damage, not a pre-merge suite run.\\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \\\"Cadence\\\" below), so a\\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). mise was moved off the apt package to the official user-local install (`~/.local/bin/mise`, 2026.11.2, owner action 2026-11-07) so both hosts share shiva's layout; toolchains in `~/.local/share/mise/installs/` were untouched. Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\\n\\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\\n\\\"nightly.clj\\\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\\nmistake, not a host outage.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\\n\\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\\ntree that did not contain them.\\n\\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\\n\\n```sh\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\\ngit fetch -q origin \\\"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\\\"\\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\\n```\\n\\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\"`). If\\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\\n\\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\\n\\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\\nfirst lane:\\n\\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\\n   only (\\\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\\\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\\n\\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\\n\\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\\n```\\n\\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\\nresource fails there immediately.\\n\\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\\n\\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\\nchange to a ledger file:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\\n```\\n\\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\\nstaging.\\n\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\n\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\n5-6-hour run.\\n\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\n\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\n\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \\\"a second UDP bind on\\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\\\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \\\"127.0.0.1\\\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\n\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\nchanges\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\"it should not be for\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\"\\n\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\n\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\\n  documented under \\\"Cadence\\\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\\n  Its results are a\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\n\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\n\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\\npre-existing by the owning seat.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\\n\\\"free laptop\\\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\\n\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\n\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\\n**Dart quick tier\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\n\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\n\\\"Patch does not apply\\\" or \\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\".\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\n\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\n\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\"run tests remotely\\nover SSH\\\" to \\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\": a heavy implementation or\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\n\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\nreference):\\n\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\"Get the code there\\\".\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\"$(mise env -s\\n   bash)\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\n   inside the same command.\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\n\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n# ==============================================================================\\n# datom.world – Development Rules & Architectural Foundations\\n# ==============================================================================\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./docs/design/datom.world.md) for master architecture, core philosophy, foundational axioms, non-negotiable invariants, code style, development workflow, and operational guides (testing, team roles, agent delegation, website formats).\\nrg: lang: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\nsrc/jolt/dao/