**NOT GATED, NOT COMMITTED** — this session has NO shell of any kind,\\\\n+so nothing could be run (no red-first reproduction, no focused suites, no kondo/cljstyle, no\\\\n+`git add`/`git commit`) and the working tree is left dirty for the next seat. Every claim below\\\\n+is source reading and reasoning; NOTHING here is firsthand runtime evidence. Read §1 first.\\\\n+\\\\n+# Findings — r10: the count domain is the engine's exact integer arithmetic, end to end\\\\n+\\\\n+## 1. The blocking limitation, stated first and without softening\\\\n+\\\\n+`tool_search` for a shell returned no `run_shell_command`, no `monitor`, no `Bash`, no\\\\n+`execute_command`. A `general-purpose`/`Explore` subagent was launched and asked to report its\\\\n+own tool inventory and to prove a shell by running `git rev-parse HEAD && git status --porcelain`;\\\\n+it reported the same absence verbatim:\\\\n+\\\\n+> **I have NO shell-execution capability of any kind.** There is no `run_shell_command`, no\\\\n+> `monitor`, no `Bash`, no `execute_command`, no `shell`, no `computer_use`, nothing that can\\\\n+> run `git`, `clojure`, or `clj-kondo`.\\\\n+\\\\n+(`computer_use` was not used as a backdoor terminal: the user has declined that install on this\\\\n+machine, per project memory. `ask_user_question` is unavailable in this non-interactive mode, so\\\\n+the open questions in §7 were decided here and are surfaced rather than asked.)\\\\n+\\\\n+Consequences, explicitly:\\\\n+\\\\n+| Required by the dispatch | Status |\\\\n+| --- | --- |\\\\n+| red-first reproduction of both residuals at the pre-fix tip | **NOT DONE** — impossible |\\\\n+| `:rc` = MAX-1 ordinary catch/return stays safe — pinned | pin WRITTEN, never executed |\\\\n+| `:rc` = 9007199254740992 pinned (Node reasoning noted) | pin WRITTEN, never executed |\\\\n+| `:rc` = `(/ 1 2)` foreign with full retention — pinned | pin WRITTEN, never executed |\\\\n+| r7 boundary controls stay green | restored in source; **green is UNVERIFIED** |\\\\n+| php-kit-parity-test + whole pin set green | **NOT RUN** |\\\\n+| 18 lane-blocked assertions byte-identical | untouched by these edits (see §5) — **not re-observed** |\\\\n+| kondo 0/0 + cljstyle clean on touched files | **NOT RUN**; formatting matched by hand to the surrounding bytes |\\\\n+| one commit on exception-php-r1r2r3, not pushed | **NOT DONE** — `git` is unavailable |\\\\n+| findings artifact | this file |\\\\n+\\\\n+The dispatch said \\\\\\\"Produce the COMPLETE deliverable in this run; do not wait for approval.\\\\\\\" The\\\\n+complete deliverable *that this session can produce* is the reviewed-and-corrected working tree\\\\n+plus this document. The commit and the gates need a seat with a shell; §6 lists the exact\\\\n+commands. **Do not treat any number in this file as a gate result — there are none.**\\\\n+\\\\n+## 2. The handover delta, hunk by hunk: adopted vs changed\\\\n+\\\\n+The interrupted session left two modified files. `git diff` was unavailable, so the delta was\\\\n+recovered two ways and cross-checked: (a) by reading the working copy against the committed\\\\n+behaviour documented in `1794488073000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r7.glm-5.3.findings.md` and against\\\\n+`1794516140000-architect-step7-recheck4.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`; then (b) against **HEAD's actual\\\\n+bytes**, which turned out to be readable after all — `/private/tmp/phpexc-review` is a detached\\\\n+worktree of this repo pinned at `265c4a4590259f2ea592cb81cd6991aa7b8afde8` (its `.git` file →\\\\n+`gitdir: /private/tmp/phpexc-review/.git`, whose `HEAD` holds the literal SHA), and\\\\n+`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.git/refs/heads/exception-php-r1r2r3` = the same SHA, confirming\\\\n+this worktree's HEAD. The r9 stdout log is a one-line `[claude-code:unrecognized model]` stub with\\\\n+no transcript and no diff, so it contributed nothing.\\\\n+\\\\n+The complete handover delta is **six hunks** — three per file, all of them reviewed below, none\\\\n+missed:\\\\n+\\\\n+- `prelude.cljc`: `host-names` :103; `php/addref` + `php/delref` :1834-1852 → :1835-1871;\\\\n+  `php/obj-val?` :2836-2903 → :2849-2943.\\\\n+- `php_exception_test.clj`: the two r7 boundary shapes deleted from\\\\n+  `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test` (HEAD :505-523) and `ratio-count` added in their slot;\\\\n+  `count-source` added; `php-count-acquisition-test` added.\\\\n+\\\\n+Against HEAD, *this round's* tree therefore differs in four places in `prelude.cljc` — the three\\\\n+handover hunks (with P4-P7 rewriting two of their comments and P6 half-reverting the third) plus\\\\n+my P8 token removal at :102 — and in three in `php_exception_test.clj` (the handover's two new\\\\n+defs kept, the deleted shapes restored). A `git diff HEAD` will show more comment churn than code\\\\n+churn; that is deliberate, because the reasoning the handover wrote down was the part that was\\\\n+wrong.\\\\n+\\\\n+One correction to an earlier assumption of mine, now settled by HEAD's bytes: **r7 had no\\\\n+near-boundary (MAX-1) shape.** r7's three added shapes were `max-long-count`, `min-long-count`\\\\n+and `non-number-count`. MAX-1 is sol's re-check4 counterexample and is pinned here for the first\\\\n+time. The two endpoint shapes are restored **byte-identical to HEAD** (payload forms included);\\\\n+only their comments were updated, because r7's rationale text named the raw host arithmetic that\\\\n+r10 replaces.\\\\n+\\\\n+### prelude.cljc\\\\n+\\\\n+| # | Hunk (working copy at handover) | Ruling | Why |\\\\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n+| P1 | `host-names` (:103) extended with `integer/integer?` | **ADOPTED verbatim** | it is an export of the `integer` module (`yin.vm.integer` `exports`, `['integer? [1] int-integer?]`), so `admit` and the linker's free-name check are satisfied wherever the profile's own `register-integer-module` ran. Required: without it, publishing the module refuses `integer/integer?` as an undeclared free name. `integer/add`/`sub`/`compare` were already declared (used at :909, :959, :1194, :1685, :5451 …). |\\\\n+| P2 | `php/addref` body: raw `(+ rc 1)` → `(integer/add … 1)` | **ADOPTED verbatim** | this is residual 1's repair. `int-add` promotes both operands with `host/promote` and demotes the result (`yin.vm.integer` :190, `dao.host.integer` `add`/`promote`/`demote`), so the JVM long maximum no longer overflows and a JS BigInt count no longer mixes with a Number `1`. |\\\\n+| P3 | `php/delref` body: raw `(- rc 1)` → `(integer/sub … 1)` | **ADOPTED verbatim** | same domain for the decrement, which is the operation the wrapper's lifetime runs. |\\\\n+| P4 | `php/addref` docstring (\\\\\\\"total over every exact-integer carrier … no bounds check remains … min-integer-bits is far beyond the native carriers\\\\\\\") | **CHANGED — rewritten** | the reasoning was wrong in a way that matters (see §3): `min-integer-bits` is a *limit*, not a safety margin, and `integer/add` answers the `::bit-limit` keyword past it. Rewritten to say what actually makes the path safe (promote/demote exactness) and why a bound alone proves nothing (MAX-1 → MAX at reception → past MAX at the lowerer's return acquisition). |\\\\n+| P5 | `php/delref` had no comment | **CHANGED — 3 lines added** | records the invariant the `(= n 0)` test depends on: `integer/sub` demotes any result inside the host's native range, so a count that reaches 0 is in the native carrier on every host and the zero test is carrier-uniform. (On JS a BigInt never `=` a Number, so this is load-bearing, not decorative.) |\\\\n+| P6 | `php/obj-val?` count check: HEAD's `data/number?` + `php/int?` + strict `integer/compare` bounds → bare `(integer/integer? (get st :rc))` | **CHANGED — half adopted, half reverted.** Kept `integer/integer?` *as the domain predicate* (that half is residual 2's repair and is correct); **restored r7's strict bounds** inside it. Final shape is HEAD's own nesting (`(let [st …]` → class check → inner `(let [rc …]`), so the code diff against 265c4a45 is one collapsed predicate level, not a restructuring | Dropping `data/number?`+`php/int?` is right and is adopted: `php/int?` is a representation classifier (it answers `:int` for a computed ratio — the re-check4 counterexample — and on JS `php/kind`'s `data/content=` fallthrough compares a BigInt count against its own float64 image, which is exactly 2^53's value, so it could classify the *required* JS-boundary count as `:float`). Dropping the bounds is wrong: see §3 (a new host-failure escape) and §4 (it inverts two committed r7 controls, which the dispatch requires to stay green). HEAD's inline comment explaining why `data/number?` had to precede `php/int?` (php/kind's non-number fallthrough calls `data/float-value` and refuses on absent counts) is replaced by one explaining why the domain predicate has to precede `integer/compare`; with `php/int?` gone, that refusal path is gone too — `integer/integer?` is total and answers false for nil. |\\\\n+| P7 | `php/obj-val?` docstring, count paragraph + \\\\\\\"Everything else\\\\\\\" paragraph | **CHANGED — rewritten** | now states both checks and the question each answers: the domain predicate makes the count *operable* for its whole ownership life; the bounds keep it inside the composition's integer *limits* and route endpoint state the safe way (foreign, retained whole, never consumed). r9's rewrite of the docstring's *opening* paragraph (extending the sweep from \\\\\\\"the reception path\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\"every ordinary acquisition after that — a caught local value returned by php source runs the lowerer's php/addref; copies and rethrows acquire too\\\\\\\") is **ADOPTED**: it is the correction re-check4 demanded, and it is accurate. |\\\\n+| P8 | *(mine, not in the handover)* `data/number?` **removed** from `host-names` (:102) | **NEW — cleanup of a dead declaration this round created** | P6 dropped the only caller: `grep number? src/cljc/yang/php` now answers just the declaration and two comments, so `host-names` (\\\\\\\"Module exports the prelude calls\\\\\\\") was declaring an export nothing calls. Statically safe, and I checked every pin that reads the list rather than assuming: `bundled-prelude-is-closed-test` is one-directional (`free-names ⊆ defined-keys ∪ host-names ∪ primitives`), so shrinking the set cannot break it and `integer/integer?` **had** to be added for it (the integer module is never in `vm/primitives`); `prelude-reaches-no-dao-host-test` needs every entry's namespace in `#{\\\\\\\"cell\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"integer\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"stream\\\\\\\"}` — `\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\"` and `\\\\\\\"integer\\\\\\\"` both qualify; `module-spec-declares-every-free-name-test` only filters the `integer/*` entries and requires each to be in the spec's primitives, which `register-integer-module` supplies for all 26 exports; `admit-refuses-a-registry-without-the-host-names-test` calls `(prelude/admit {})`, which still refuses on the remaining names. `portable_harness/host-registry` registers the four modules wholesale and then admits, so it never enumerated the list either. If a gate I could not run contradicts this, restoring the one token is the whole revert. |\\\\n+\\\\n+Unchanged by me and walked as consumers (r7's sweep, re-verified against the current bytes):\\\\n+`php/finalize!` (:4341-4353) still holds a raw `(- (get st :rc) 1)`. It is **safe and was left\\\\n+alone**: the line immediately before it pins the cell to the literal `:rc 1`, so the only\\\\n+increments the raw subtraction can ever see are acquisitions made during the destructor, and\\\\n+reaching the long maximum from 1 would take 2^63 of them. Converting it would be an untestable\\\\n+edit to the destroy path for no reachable gain; disclosed here so the next re-check does not\\\\n+have to re-derive it. `php/rc-of`, `php/dup-slot`'s `(= (get st :rc) 1)` and `php/shutdown-pass`\\\\n+are equality/reads, total. `php.rt/mint-retained` (:4706ff) mints `:rc 1` — engine-owned\\\\n+complete state. `lower-return` (`lower.cljc` :2945) wraps every returned expression in\\\\n+`php/addref`, which is now the exact domain: that is the second acquisition in sol's\\\\n+counterexample and it is closed by P2.\\\\n+\\\\n+### test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj\\\\n+\\\\n+| # | Hunk | Ruling | Why |\\\\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n+| T1 | `count-source` (new `^:private` def): `<?php function catch_return($f) { try { return $f(); } catch (Throwable $e) { return $e; } }` | **ADOPTED** (docstring rewritten) | sol's own receiver source, verbatim in behaviour: an ordinary catch/return with no numeric operation anywhere, whose `return $e` runs the lowerer's acquisition. The harness pattern is already proven in this file — `run-egress-observation` passes its own `egress-source` to `kit/compose-exception` the same way, and two green tests use it. |\\\\n+| T2 | `php-count-acquisition-test` (new deftest): per-shape bindings → `catch_return` → `{:…-returned :…-identical}` | **ADOPTED structure; CHANGED shape set 4 → 2** | kept `near-max` (9223372036854775806) and `js-number-boundary` (9007199254740992) — the two counterexamples sol asked to add. **Removed `max` and `min`**: with the bounds restored they are foreign, not local, and they are pinned as foreign in T3 where the full retention observations live. Asserting them local here would have contradicted the dispatch's \\\\\\\"the r7 boundary controls stay green\\\\\\\". |\\\\n+| T3 | `max-long-count` / `min-long-count` shapes **deleted** from `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test` | **REVERTED — both restored, byte-identical to HEAD :505-523** (payload forms included; only their comment blocks updated, since r7's rationale named the raw host arithmetic r10 replaces) | these are r7's committed boundary controls (11 shapes, 44 keys, green at 265c4a45). Deleting them and re-asserting the opposite disposition elsewhere is a pin inversion, which every prior re-check has treated as a weakening. Restored in r7's position (after `string-class-no-rc`, before `non-number-count`). |\\\\n+| T4 | `ratio-count` shape added (`:rc (/ 1 2)`) | **ADOPTED verbatim** | the residual-2 pin sol required: computed rational, admitted as FOREIGN with the complete condition, its evidence and original payload identity (the four standard observations). Comment re-stamped r9 → r10. |\\\\n+| T5 | `non-number-count` shape kept but displaced after `ratio-count` | **ADOPTED — restored to its HEAD position** | r6's rule; back in r7's committed order, with `ratio-count` appended last → twelve shapes, 48 keys, still ONE composed program (the shape list drives `bindings`, `entries` and `expected`, so the expected map is computed, not hand-counted). |\\\\n+| T6 | deftest comment \\\\\\\"All eight shapes run in ONE composed program … catches the 8th consecutive per-shape run\\\\\\\" | **CHANGED** | stale since r7 (sol flagged it in re-check4: \\\\\\\"The old 'all eight' comment is stale\\\\\\\"). Now says twelve, and records what the r7 endpoint shapes pin and why r10 keeps them. |\\\\n+| T7 | comment \\\\\\\"addref's `(+ rc 1)` consumes it\\\\\\\" | **CHANGED** | the code no longer says that; now \\\\\\\"addref's count acquisition consumes it\\\\\\\". |\\\\n+| T8 | everything else in the file (`php-kit-parity-test`, `php-kit-batch-parity-test`, the directed legs, the forgery/provenance/nil tests, `egress-source`, `injected`, `boundary-call`) | **UNTOUCHED** | byte-identical, so the disclosed lane-blocked assertions cannot have moved. |\\\\n+\\\\n+## 3. The defect I found in the handover that neither r9 nor re-check4 identified\\\\n+\\\\n+The handover's admission was `(integer/integer? rc)` and nothing else, on the stated reasoning\\\\n+that \\\\\\\"counts at or beyond the native carriers are inside php's own integer model …\\\\n+(min-integer-bits is far beyond the native carriers) … the arithmetic itself cannot overflow\\\\\\\".\\\\n+`integer/add` is exact, but it is **not total**: it is *limited*.\\\\n+\\\\n+```clojure\\\\n+;; yin.vm.integer\\\\n+(defn- out [limits b] (if (> (bits b) (::max-bits limits)) ::bit-limit (host/demote b)))\\\\n+(defn- int-add [limits a b] (out limits (host/add (big! 'add 0 a) (big! 'add 1 b))))\\\\n+(defn- big! [op arg x] (if (host/exact-integer? x) (host/promote x) (wrong-type! op arg :integer)))\\\\n+```\\\\n+\\\\n+and `::integer/max-bits` is finite for every PHP composition: the profile's floor\\\\n+`yang.php.antlr.prelude/min-integer-bits` is **4096** (`register-integer-module` refuses\\\\n+anything less), and the compositions these tests build install **100000** —\\\\n+`test/yang/php/antlr/portable_harness.cljc:78`, `{::integer/max-bits 100000,\\\\n+::integer/max-digits 4300}`. Whichever number applies, it is the point past which `integer/add`\\\\n+stops answering an integer and answers the keyword `:yin.vm.integer/bit-limit` instead. Meanwhile\\\\n+the VM's arithmetic primitives are raw host references (`yin.vm/primitives`: `(pure '+ + …)`,\\\\n+`(pure '- - …)`, `(pure '* * …)`), which no `::max-bits` bounds and which auto-promote on the\\\\n+JVM — the same fact that let sol build a Ratio with `(/ 1 2)`. So a guest can build an\\\\n+arbitrarily wide exact-integer count in a handful of operations (`(* x x)` from the long maximum:\\\\n+63 → 126 → 252 → … bits, i.e. past the 4096 floor in six squarings and past 100000 bits in\\\\n+eleven), and then:\\\\n+\\\\n+1. `integer/integer?` admits it (a `clojure.lang.BigInt` *is* an exact-integer carrier);\\\\n+2. reception's `php/addref` calls `integer/add`, which answers `::bit-limit`;\\\\n+3. that **keyword is `assoc`'d into the cell as `:rc`** and the payload is raised as a local\\\\n+   Throwable — no refusal, nothing observable yet;\\\\n+4. the next ownership operation on that count (`php/delref`'s `integer/sub`, or any\\\\n+   `integer/compare`) reaches `big!` with a keyword and throws `ex-info` `\\\\\\\"integer primitive\\\\n+   refused\\\\\\\"` `{:yin.vm.integer/reason :wrong-type, :op sub, :arg 0, :expected :integer}` — a\\\\n+   **host failure escaping the boundary**, which is precisely the class this whole round exists\\\\n+   to eliminate (\\\\\\\"a caller checks for them before the call, so reaching one is a defect of the\\\\n+   caller, and it stays a host failure, never a guest exception\\\\\\\").\\\\n+\\\\n+Restoring the strict bounds closes this by construction: every admitted count is < 2^63 in\\\\n+magnitude, so it is at most 63 bits and every acquisition result at most ~64 bits — 64 is below\\\\n+any `::max-bits` this profile can be registered under, so `integer/add`/`integer/sub` can never\\\\n+reach `out`'s limit branch on the ownership path, and `big!` can never see a non-integer because\\\\n+admission already proved the carrier. The admitted domain is then a strict subset of the domain\\\\n+the arithmetic is total over, which is what \\\\\\\"match admitted count carriers and all ordinary\\\\n+ownership operations to one safe arithmetic domain\\\\\\\" has to mean; the unbounded version matched\\\\n+them to a domain where the arithmetic is only *conditionally* total.\\\\n+\\\\n+**This is reasoning from source, not a reproduction.** I could not run it. It is falsifiable in\\\\n+one command by the next seat (§6, step 1): set `:rc` to an exact integer wider than the\\\\n+composition's `::integer/max-bits` (100000 in these lanes, 4096 at the profile floor) at the\\\\n+handover's admission and watch the second ownership operation refuse.\\\\n+\\\\n+## 4. Why the bounds also settle the rule-1 / rule-3 question (and the dispatch's fourth bullet)\\\\n+\\\\n+`docs/design/yang.antlr.md` :3868-3876:\\\\n+\\\\n+> 1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that runtime's ordinary\\\\n+>    catching and identity behavior.\\\\n+> 3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP, represents an otherwise\\\\n+>    foreign payload with its own `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and\\\\n+>    original payload.\\\\n+\\\\n+r9 read rule 1 as making the endpoints local (\\\\\\\"a handle whose count is the native maximum … is\\\\n+VALID local state\\\\\\\"). The dispatch reads the other way, and so does the code: it requires\\\\n+MAX-1 and 2^53 to be pinned *safe through an ordinary catch/return* and separately requires\\\\n+\\\\\\\"the r7 boundary controls stay green\\\\\\\" — and r7's boundary controls assert that the two\\\\n+endpoints are foreign. Those two requirements are only simultaneously satisfiable with the\\\\n+bounds kept, because reception's acquisition is what moves MAX-1 *onto* MAX: the endpoint is\\\\n+excluded at admission, the count just below it is admitted and then leaves the range safely\\\\n+under exact arithmetic. That is also the honest reading of rule 1/rule 3 — a count at the exact\\\\n+endpoint of php's own int encoding is not state php's machinery could have produced and still\\\\n+operate on inside that encoding, so it is \\\\\\\"otherwise foreign\\\\\\\" and is retained whole (rule 3),\\\\n+while every count php *could* have produced keeps its identity through unlimited acquisitions\\\\n+(rule 1). No broad host catch was added; nothing is caught after the fact.\\\\n+\\\\n+## 5. Pin map — each required state, and where it is pinned\\\\n+\\\\n+| Required state (dispatch) | Where | Observations |\\\\n+| --- | --- | --- |\\\\n+| `:rc` = MAX-1 through an ordinary catch/return stays SAFE (no overflow) | `php-count-acquisition-test`, shape `near-max` | `-returned` = route `:return` (at the pre-fix tip this leg dies in `lower-return`'s acquisition with `Math.addExact` \\\\\\\"long overflow\\\\\\\"); `-identical` = `(= payload value)`, payload identity preserved |\\\\n+| `:rc` = 9007199254740992 (JS BigInt boundary; JVM-verifiable as a number, Node reasoning noted) | same test, shape `js-number-boundary` | same two observations. The reasoning is recorded in `count-source`'s docstring: a plain Long in this JVM lane (where it is verified), a BigInt carrier on JS where the same decimal parses past the safe-Number range and a raw `(+ rc 1)` throws `TypeError: Cannot mix BigInt and other types` (sol's Node primitive diagnostic, both signs). The tri-host acceptance lane covers Node; **this laptop lane cannot and does not claim to.** |\\\\n+| `:rc` = `(/ 1 2)` admitted as FOREIGN with full retention map | `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test`, shape `ratio-count` | all four per-shape observations: `-retained` (route `:return`, the wrapper came back), `-local` (`is_foreign` on the wrapper), `-condition` (`later` reads `$saved` and compares the WHOLE condition map, `:yang.call/data {:complete [:evidence]}` included), `-payload` (original payload identity inside the retained condition) |\\\\n+| the r7 boundary controls stay green | same test, shapes `max-long-count`, `min-long-count` — restored | same four observations each |\\\\n+| missing / non-number / legitimate-local controls kept | `string-class-no-rc`, `non-number-count`, `authentic-cell-bad-class` (`:rc 1`, re-check3's coverage note), `php-local-reception-directed-test`, plus the two new local count shapes | unchanged |\\\\n+| kit parity + existing pin set | untouched tests | **NOT RUN** |\\\\n+| 18 lane-blocked assertions byte-identical | `php-kit-batch-parity-test` and the fixtures were not edited (T8) | **not re-observed** — no shell |\\\\n+\\\\n+Twelve shapes × 4 observations = a 48-key expected map per backend (`:semantic`, `:stack`,\\\\n+`:register`), computed from the shape list, all in ONE composed program.\\\\n+\\\\n+Deliberately NOT added, disclosed: a **float-count** foreign shape. re-check4 says \\\\\\\"Keep the\\\\n+missing/non-number, float, boundary and legitimate local controls\\\\\\\", but no float-count shape\\\\n+exists at 265c4a45 to keep (r7's eleven are enumerated in r7's findings), and every way of\\\\n+encoding one in the observation language is either host-divergent (a bare integral `1.0`: JS\\\\n+cannot distinguish it from `1`, which is why the prelude builds floats with `data/float64`) or\\\\n+unverifiable from here (whether a `1.5` literal / `data/float64` application survives the kit's\\\\n+observation-form emission). Because all twelve shapes share ONE composed program and ONE\\\\n+expected map, a single mis-emitting shape would red the restored r7 controls along with it — an\\\\n+unacceptable bet with no way to run it. The exact-integer predicate already rejects every\\\\n+non-integral carrier by construction, and `ratio-count` pins the case that fooled both\\\\n+`data/number?` and `php/int?`. **Suggested for the next seat, which can run it:** add\\\\n+`[\\\\\\\"float-count\\\\\\\" …]` with a `data/float64`-built non-integral count and confirm all four\\\\n+observations.\\\\n+\\\\n+## 6. What the next seat must run (in this order), and the exact commit\\\\n+\\\\n+Nothing below was executed here. Each command foreground, explicit timeout, nothing backgrounded,\\\\n+focused namespaces only; `yang.call.exception-contract-test` NEVER locally (shiva-only).\\\\n+\\\\n+```sh\\\\n+cd /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-phpexc\\\\n+git status --porcelain          # expect exactly: M prelude.cljc, M php_exception_test.clj (+ untracked collab/)\\\\n+git diff --stat HEAD            # sanity: two files\\\\n+\\\\n+# 1. RED-FIRST for §3 (do this before anything else; it is the only new claim needing proof).\\\\n+#    At the HANDOVER admission (integer/integer? alone) build a :rc wider than the composition's\\\\n+#    ::integer/max-bits, e.g. by squaring the long maximum with the VM's raw host `*` (six\\\\n+#    squarings clear the 4096 profile floor, eleven clear these lanes' 100000), and drive it\\\\n+#    through reception plus one ordinary acquisition. Expect: the count becomes\\\\n+#    :yin.vm.integer/bit-limit and the next ownership op throws \\\\\\\"integer primitive refused\\\\\\\"\\\\n+#    {:reason :wrong-type, :op sub}.\\\\n+#    Then confirm the same payload under THIS tree is foreign with complete retention.\\\\n+# 2. RED-FIRST for the two residuals at 265c4a45 (sol's counterexamples), so the chronology is\\\\n+#    real: MAX-1 through catch/return -> \\\\\\\"long overflow\\\\\\\"; (/ 1 2) -> integer/compare :wrong-type.\\\\n+\\\\n+timeout 900 clojure -M:test -e \\\\\\\\\\\\n+ '(do (require (quote [clojure.test :as t])) \\\\\\\\\\\\n+      (require (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)) \\\\\\\\\\\\n+      (t/run-tests (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)))'\\\\n+# the twelve focused namespaces r7 used, at minimum:\\\\n+#   yang.call.php-exception-test, yang.php.antlr.prelude-test, yang.php.antlr.class-model-test,\\\\n+#   yang.php.antlr.objects-test, yang.php.antlr.generators-test,\\\\n+#   yang.php.antlr.builtin-surfaces-test, yang.php.antlr.e2e-fast-test,\\\\n+#   yang.php.antlr.exception-source-test, dao.host.conformance-test, yin.vm.integer-test\\\\n+# yang.php.antlr.prelude-test is the one that guards P8 (the host-names change): its four static\\\\n+# pins read that list. If any of them goes red, restore the `data/number?` token and re-run — the\\\\n+# rest of this round does not depend on that cleanup.\\\\n+timeout 300 clojure -M:clj-kondo --lint src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\\\\\\\n+                                        test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj   # expect 0/0\\\\n+timeout 300 clojure -M:cljstyle check src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\\\\\\\n+                                      test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj     # expect clean\\\\n+git add src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj \\\\\\\\\\\\n+        collab/exception-track/1794518915000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r10.qwen3.8-max.findings.md\\\\n+git commit -m \\\\\\\"fix(yang.php): the count domain is the engine's exact integer arithmetic end to end (r10, sol's re-check4 completion)\\\\\\\"\\\\n+# DO NOT PUSH — the orchestrator pushes after review coordination.\\\\n+```\\\\n+\\\\n+If `cljstyle check` reports anything, run `cljstyle fix` on the two files and re-run the probes.\\\\n+The formatting risk is small because almost nothing here is newly hand-formatted: `php/obj-val?`\\\\n+keeps HEAD's own nesting and indentation (one predicate level collapsed out of it), the two\\\\n+restored shapes are HEAD's bytes, and the only reflowed line is r9's wrapped `cell/set!` in\\\\n+`php/addref` (its continuations align to `assoc`'s first argument and to `do`'s body column, which\\\\n+is what cljstyle would produce). Nothing was written to either file except through targeted\\\\n+edits, so no whole-file reformat landed.\\\\n+\\\\n+To diff the working tree against HEAD without a shell of your own, `/private/tmp/phpexc-review`\\\\n+still holds a detached checkout of `265c4a45` (if it has not been cleaned up): reading the same\\\\n+path in both trees gives the exact delta. `git diff HEAD` in this worktree is authoritative once\\\\n+you have a shell.\\\\n+\\\\n+## 7. Open questions I had to decide alone (`ask_user_question` is unavailable in this mode)\\\\n+\\\\n+1. **Endpoints: local or foreign?** Decided FOREIGN (bounds restored) — §4. This is the one\\\\n+   judgement call that reverses a handover hunk, and it is the one the orchestrator should look\\\\n+   at first. If the owner/architect rules that rule 1 makes the endpoints valid local state, the\\\\n+   revert is small: drop the two `integer/compare` clauses in `php/obj-val?`, move\\\\n+   `max-long-count`/`min-long-count` back into `php-count-acquisition-test`, and accept §3's\\\\n+   `::bit-limit` escape (which would then need a different closure — e.g. refusing a count whose\\\\n+   `integer/add` answers a keyword, which is a check on the result rather than the domain).\\\\n+2. **`php/finalize!`'s raw `(- rc 1)`**: left alone, with the safety argument recorded in §2.\\\\n+3. **float-count shape**: not added, §5.\\\\n+4. **Commit**: not made. Leaving the tree dirty is deliberate — a commit I cannot gate would put\\\\n+   an unverified r10 in history under a message claiming a completion I did not observe. The next\\\\n+   seat can `git add`/`commit` in one step once §6's gates are green.\\\\n+\\\\n+## 8. Residuals and carry-overs (unchanged, not re-litigated here)\\\\n+\\\\n+Steward-owned: the §3a instrument/lane conflict (the lane-blocked fixtures), the dao.jing\\\\n+laptop-lane stall, the lifecycle instrument preference, suspension/context-lifetime obligations,\\\\n+and the remote/foundation merge gates — the contract test, `test:changed`, the 12-pair matrix and\\\\n+the Node/Dart harnesses. re-check4's coverage note on the committed nil pin (it checks retain +\\\\n+`is_foreign == false` but not the exact TypeError class or provenance absence) still stands:\\\\n+`php-nil-condition-reception-test` was not extended in this round, because extending it is a new\\\\n+pin I cannot run. Per project memory, this round's local green — had there been any — would not\\\\n+have moved ledger rows: acceptance is the shiva-only contract kit deciding at the PUBLIC entries,\\\\n+and everything above is a different instrument.\\\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc b/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\\\\nindex 99f14d83..d10c8b01 100644\\\\n--- a/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\\\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\\\\n@@ -99,8 +99,8 @@\\\\n            data/count data/nth data/subvec data/str-concat\\\\n            data/str-length data/str-join data/str-compare data/str->code-points\\\\n            data/code-points->str data/substring data/float64 data/float-value\\\\n-           data/content= data/number? data/dissoc data/into\\\\n-           integer/add integer/sub integer/neg integer/mul integer/compare\\\\n+           data/content= data/dissoc data/into\\\\n+           integer/integer? integer/add integer/sub integer/neg integer/mul integer/compare\\\\n            integer/to-float integer/compare-float integer/true-div integer/pow\\\\n            integer/shift-left integer/shift-right integer/bit-and integer/bit-or\\\\n            integer/bit-xor integer/bit-not integer/from-float integer/quot-rem\\\\n@@ -1832,21 +1832,41 @@\\\\n        (let [c (php/cell-of x)]\\\\n          (if (nil? c) false (= (get (cell/get c) :released?) true))))]\\\\n     [php/addref\\\\n+     ;; the count arithmetic is the engine's EXACT-INTEGER arithmetic, not\\\\n+     ;; the raw host +/-: integer/add promotes both operands to the host\\\\n+     ;; big integer and demotes the result, so it is exact over every\\\\n+     ;; exact-integer carrier on every supported host (JVM longs and big\\\\n+     ;; carriers, JS safe numbers and BigInts, Dart ints and BigInts) —\\\\n+     ;; no native overflow at the long maximum and no BigInt/Number mixing\\\\n+     ;; on JS. That is what makes a count safe for its WHOLE ownership\\\\n+     ;; life instead of for one step: admission bounds it strictly inside\\\\n+     ;; ±2^63, but reception's acquisition puts a count one below the\\\\n+     ;; maximum AT the maximum, and the next ordinary acquisition — the\\\\n+     ;; lowerer wraps every returned expression in php/addref, and copies\\\\n+     ;; and rethrows acquire too — puts it past, where the raw host +\\\\n+     ;; throws. The fast kernels keep the common small-count path native;\\\\n+     ;; php/obj-val? admits exactly this domain, integer/integer? being\\\\n+     ;; the domain predicate of these primitives.\\\\n      (fn [x]\\\\n        (let [c (php/cell-of x)]\\\\n          (if (nil? c)\\\\n            x\\\\n            (if (php/released? x)\\\\n              x\\\\n-             (do (cell/set! c (assoc (cell/get c) :rc (+ (get (cell/get c) :rc) 1))) x)))))]\\\\n+             (do (cell/set! c (assoc (cell/get c)\\\\n+                                     :rc (integer/add (get (cell/get c) :rc) 1)))\\\\n+                 x)))))]\\\\n     [php/delref\\\\n+     ;; the same exact domain as addref. A count only ever reaches 0 in\\\\n+     ;; the native carrier (integer/sub demotes a result inside the host's\\\\n+     ;; native range), so the zero test below is carrier-uniform.\\\\n      (fn [x]\\\\n        (let [c (php/cell-of x)]\\\\n          (if (nil? c)\\\\n            :php/null\\\\n            (if (php/released? x)\\\\n              :php/null\\\\n-             (let [n (- (get (cell/get c) :rc) 1)]\\\\n+             (let [n (integer/sub (get (cell/get c) :rc) 1)]\\\\n                (do (cell/set! c (assoc (cell/get c) :rc n))\\\\n                    (if (= n 0) (php/destroy x) :php/null)))))))]\\\\n     [php/release-slots\\\\n@@ -2837,30 +2857,47 @@\\\\n      ;; §8.10.7 reception safety: is `v` a value php's own machinery could\\\\n      ;; have produced as an object, safe for EVERY operation the local\\\\n      ;; reception path performs — instance-of's class read AND the\\\\n-     ;; ownership acquisition that follows it? Reception runs\\\\n-     ;; `(php/raise (php/addref v))`; addref's cell-of/slot-kind selects\\\\n-     ;; its cell by :php/ref FIRST, then :php/arr, then :php/obj — so a\\\\n-     ;; payload carrying a competing marker (a genuine object handle with\\\\n-     ;; a forged or even genuine :php/ref; a map error with :php/ref 42)\\\\n-     ;; would be validated as one representation and consumed as another.\\\\n-     ;; Anything with a non-nil :php/ref or :php/arr marker is FOREIGN\\\\n-     ;; here, whatever else it carries. The two admitted shapes and the\\\\n-     ;; state each downstream consumer reads:\\\\n+     ;; ownership acquisition that follows it — and every ordinary\\\\n+     ;; acquisition after that (a caught local value returned by php\\\\n+     ;; source runs the lowerer's php/addref; copies and rethrows acquire\\\\n+     ;; too)? Anything with a non-nil :php/ref or :php/arr marker is\\\\n+     ;; FOREIGN here, whatever else it carries (slot-kind selects those\\\\n+     ;; keys first, so a competing marker would be validated as one\\\\n+     ;; representation and consumed as another). The two admitted shapes\\\\n+     ;;