which checks the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThrowable hierarchy and uses the repaired exact addref. Only boundary reception changes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nits class/identity. An ordinary catch retaining one more reference cannot make an otherwise\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nunchanged local exception foreign.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Endpoint ruling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nSection 8.10.7 rule 1 and the owner ruling v2 R2 require already-local exceptions to retain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nordinary catching and identity. Rule 3 applies to an OTHERWISE-foreign payload; it does\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot authorize reclassifying this runtime-produced state just because its private reference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncount reached the source-language integer endpoint.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe accepted Python implementation at prelude.cljc:1067-1079 recognizes its own exception\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrepresentation/hierarchy and raises that value unchanged. It has no PHP reference-count\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nanalogue that would justify a signed-64-bit cutoff on PHP'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s now-exact ownership arithmetic.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPHP still needs representation and operability checks; that does not make every previous\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncount cutoff a semantic invariant after its original arithmetic reason has been removed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nI therefore rule the other way on the handover question: the signed-64-bit endpoints,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nby themselves, are not a reason to wrap an otherwise admitted Throwable under the repaired\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\narithmetic. The positive maximum counterexample alone is enough to reject the restored\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npolicy. The minimum-only exclusion likewise lacks the old decrement-overflow justification;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nany separately proposed count invariant must be justified consistently, not inferred from\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe old host carrier limits.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRequired completion:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Preserve exact integer/add and integer/sub and the exact-integer admission predicate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Make reception preserve local type/identity across ordinary acquisitions and later\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  receptions, including the runtime-produced endpoint and beyond-native results.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Extend the near-max source pin to two calls while the first result remains live, with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  original identity and original Exception class on BOTH results.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Keep endpoint coverage, but revise its asserted disposition under this ruling instead\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  of deleting the cases. Keep malformed, competing-marker, ratio, missing/non-number and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  legitimate-local controls with complete-condition/evidence/payload checks for foreign\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  cases.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis revises the old endpoint disposition explicitly in response to the requested ruling;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nit is not permission to silently invert or remove unrelated pins.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Do not apply the documented bare revert without a limit-aware replacement\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe engineer is correct about the separate integer-module limit hazard: integer/integer?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrecognizes carriers without checking ::integer/max-bits; integer/add and integer/sub can\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreturn ::bit-limit. Installing that keyword as :rc would make the next integer operation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrefuse. Merely deleting both bounds and accepting EVERY exact integer is not a complete fix.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat hazard does not justify the particular strict 64-bit boundary: MAX, MAX+1 and MIN are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwell inside the profile'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s minimum 4096-bit arithmetic limit. Count validation/limit handling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmust distinguish genuinely unsupported state from the local results ordinary ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnow produces. No broad host catch is authorized.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTwo details in the findings'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\'' supporting argument should also be corrected:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(1) raw clojure.core/* on two Long operands does not auto-promote past overflow; a BigInt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nseed is needed for the proposed repeated-squaring construction; (2) an initial 63-bit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbound does not prove ALL subsequent ownership results stay 64-bit. Neither correction\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnegates the real ::bit-limit hazard, and neither is the blocking reproduction above.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Ruling 1 — both re-check-4 count-domain counterexamples are repaired\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nphp/addref (:1856-1857) and php/delref (:1869) now use the integer module. Its checked big\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npath promotes operands to the host big carrier, computes exactly, and demotes to the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncanonical carrier; its fast path falls back when the safe native result range is exceeded.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe ordinary lower-return acquisition still calls php/addref (lower.cljc:2945), so the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrepair applies after reception as required, including copying/rethrow operations using it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe source receiver above now completes its FIRST near-max call with original identity;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe previous native-long overflow is closed. The 2\\\\\\\"'^53 decimal also completes with original'\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nidentity through both calls on the JVM.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nFor JS, dao.host.integer/exact-integer? admits BigInt, promote keeps both arithmetic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\noperands in BigInt form, and demote produces the canonical Number/BigInt result. Thus the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncount is no longer sent to raw BigInt-plus-Number arithmetic. integer/sub demotes zero to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe native zero, preserving the existing (= n 0) destruction test. This is verified by\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreading the actual host/module implementations; no complete Node/Dart runtime green is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclaimed by this review. The decimal in the JVM pin is a Long and alone would not prove JS.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAt :2936 integer/integer? now precedes BOTH integer/compare calls. Its implementation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndelegates directly to host/exact-integer?, so a computed JVM Ratio, float64 content, a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDouble, nil, or a non-number never reaches those comparisons. The old representation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclassifier php/int? and the broad data/number? check are gone from count admission.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nFirsthand direct reception controls, including full evidence and payload identity:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Count expression | ForeignException | Original local identity | Whole condition retained | Original payload retained |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| (/ 1 2) | true | false | true | true |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| (data/float64 1) | true | false | true | true |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| (data/float-value 1) | true | false | true | true |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| (/ 6 3) | false | true | n/a | n/a |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| parse MAX | true | false | true | true |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| parse MIN | true | false | true | true |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe last two rows record CURRENT behavior, which the endpoint ruling above rejects;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe retention machinery itself works on those rejected paths. The computed-ratio\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nresidual is CLOSED, not merely hidden behind another refusal.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Ruling 3 — accept leaving finalize\\\\\\\"'!'\\\\\\\"'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s raw unpin in this round\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAt prelude.cljc:4382 finalize\\\\\\\"'! overwrites :rc with the literal native 1 BEFORE invoking'\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe destructor. The raw subtraction at :4386 therefore does not consume the large count\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nadmitted from a boundary. Under ordinary PHP ownership, only acquisitions/releases made\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nduring this destructor contribute to its unpin value.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nI accept that scoped argument for the requested residual: the near-max and JS-BigInt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreceived-carrier counterexamples cannot flow into this subtraction. No conversion is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrequired for this review. This is not a proof against arbitrary guest cell/set\\\\\\\"'! corruption'\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nduring a destructor, nor an unrestricted totality claim for an unbounded number of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nacquisitions. Neither is the counterexample established here.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Ruling 4 — pins, publication surface and scope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe requested range is one commit and exactly three changed files: the PHP prelude,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe PHP exception test, and the r10 findings. No other frontend, VM implementation,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlowerer, shared kit, fixture, ledger, or design file changes in this range.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNo previous assertion or payload case is removed. The two original endpoint payload\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nforms remain, with their foreign-retention observations; the computed-ratio case and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe ordinary catch/return acquisition test are additive. The committed shape matrix is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnow TWELVE cases with FOUR observations each (48 expected keys per backend), rather than\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe supplied gate summary'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s 45-key description. I did not inspect that remote batch'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nraw output and do not equate these cardinalities; the direct ratio result above is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfirsthand evidence of the repaired ratio path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAdding integer/integer? to host-names declares an already-existing integer module export,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot a new PHP publication door. Removing data/number? removes an unused declaration.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe PHP exports, aliases, reserved provenance doors, receiver/mint privacy and mint call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ngraph are unchanged. There is no broad host catch or arbitrary-condition mint helper.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\naddref/delref are outside the original receiver'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s textual region, but changing ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\noperations is expressly required by re-check 4'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s whole-consumption-path residual and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthis dispatch. No unrelated ownership algorithm changed. The literal surface-registry\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfile 1794343000000 remains absent here, as disclosed in the original review; scope is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nchecked against the available binding briefs and the actual diff, not a nonexistent\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nregistry artifact.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Evidence and remaining gate disposition\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nFirsthand this round:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Full requested source/test diff, re-check 4, r10 findings, governing architecture,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  §8.10.7, owner ruling v2, accepted Python reception and relevant integer/ownership/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  lowering implementations inspected.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Bounded current-source JVM diagnostics completed with exit 0: direct count controls\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  and the parsed/lowered receiver called twice with three count controls.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Static surface/adoption/reachability pins: 3 tests, 14 passes, 0 failures, 0 errors.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- git diff --check 265c4a45..59329a05 passed; tracked working-tree status remained clean.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe source probe borrowed only existing generated parser class directories from the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nphpexc checkout, plus cached dependency jars; current worktree source/test roots led\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe classpath. PHP manifest.edn and PhpParser.g4 match that checkout byte-for-byte.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNo builds, generated files, dependency resolution or cache writes were performed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTwo preliminary source-probe attempts had diagnostic-script setup/read errors; the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreported source table is from the corrected, successful bounded invocation, not those\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nattempts. The parserless identity failure was independently observed as well.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe orchestrator'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''s tip gate evidence in the dispatch is accepted as supplied evidence:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfocused count/nil/shape/directed/adoption/reachability batches, listed namespaces, kondo\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand cljstyle. Those successes cover the existing assertions; a one-reception count pin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndoes not exercise the newly demonstrated second reception. I did not rerun the linked\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbatches, contract kit, Node/Dart boundary harness, full directed matrix or foundation gate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe three disclosed :php/local-reception :payload-identical lane-blocked failures retain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntheir previous disposition; no changed shared fixture/expectation contributes to the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnew failure. Existing nil-pin class/provenance coverage notes, suspension/context lifetime\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand steward-owned remote/foundation obligations remain as previously recorded.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY.** The arithmetic and computed-ratio repairs stand.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nComplete stable local identity at subsequent reception before this PHP step-7 slice closes.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",encoding=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"utf-8\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\nprint(str(p));print(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"lines\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",len(p.read_text().splitlines()))'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"/private/tmp/phpexc-review/collab/exception-track/1794527358000-architect-step7-recheck5.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\\\\nlines 231\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n61:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_31\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"