packet around each result, or new\\nlanguage-specific return instruction.\\n\\nAn escaping source exception invokes the supplied raise exit with:\\n\\n```clojure\\n{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown\\n :yang.call/value  original-thrown-value}\\n```\\n\\nThis is the argument to an exceptional continuation transfer, not an\\nordinary return. Returning an identical-looking map returns normal data.\\nLikewise, a source program throwing a map with these keys throws that\\nmap as the **payload of a new condition**; its shape is never evidence\\nthat it is already a condition to forward. No engine-effect value is\\nsynthesized.\\n\\nProtocol failures use qualified reasons such as\\n`:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`, and\\n`:yang.call/unsupported-binding`, with plain evidence. The caller maps\\nthem to a documented local exception form retaining the complete\\noriginal condition. Callee argument-binding failures retain the actual\\ncallee exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. These reasons do not extend\\n`dao.stream`'s outcome algebra.\\n\\nThe receiving caller interprets an escaping exception as follows:\\n\\n1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that\\n   runtime's ordinary catching and identity behavior.\\n2. A runtime permitting arbitrary thrown values, such as JavaScript,\\n   may deliver the original payload directly.\\n3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP,\\n   represents an otherwise foreign payload with its own\\n   `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original\\n   payload. Catch-all catches this local object. Typed source catches\\n   match its local hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names.\\n\\n`ForeignException` is a proposed prelude-owned type, not a VM type.\\nUnchanged rethrow propagates the original condition without adding\\nwrappers. Raising a new exception creates a new condition. Crossing back\\ninto a runtime recognizing the original exception restores its original\\ntype and identity. Formatting a foreign payload must not implicitly\\ninvoke a foreign method.\\n\\nThe runtime associates its generated wrapper's identity with the retained\\ncondition for the wrapper's entire lifetime, separate from source-writable\\nattributes. The association normally lives in private wrapper-cell\\ncontents, or an equivalent task-owned identity structure with the same\\nlifetime. It survives storing or returning the wrapper after the creating\\ncall/catch activation has ended, and remains recoverable when that same\\nwrapper is rethrown in another activation. Temporary catch state alone is\\ninsufficient. Reachability/reclamation must retain the condition and its\\npayload while the wrapper is live, without an ambient registry.\\n\\nThe runtime must not detect wrappers or unwrap arbitrary maps by their\\nkey names. JavaScript may expose raw payloads for\\n`:yang.call/thrown`; its local exception for a protocol failure must still\\nretain that failure's qualified condition. Rethrowing the same generated\\nexception forwards that complete condition, including protocol reasons;\\nthrowing an unrelated value creates a new `:yang.call/thrown` condition.\\nSaved activation state refers to this identity-owned association; exiting\\nor restoring a catch does not destroy it.\\n\\nVM application/custody defects and host implementation failures remain\\noperational failures. Do not catch them broadly and relabel them as\\nsource exceptions. Existing explicit stream/module refusals keep their\\nsource mappings under the relevant profiles.\\n\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:431:(defn float-value\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:438:    (wrong-type! 'float-value 0 :number))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:446:(defn float64\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:453:  (cbor/float64 (float-value x)))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:530:   ['float-value [1] float-value]\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:47:   integers through `dao.host.integer`, the Float64, Decimal and Rational\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:84:   `Float64` carrier refusing implicit numeric coercion) carries the same\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:212:(defn float64?\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:214:   Float64 carrier or a non-integral, non-finite or negative-zero number\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:219:     :cljs (or (instance? number/Float64 x)\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:242:(defn float64\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:244:   the JVM, the Float64 carrier on JavaScript (so integral floats such as\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:250:(defn float64-from-bits\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:332:    (float64? x) (let [[neg e f] (float/fields (number/float-value x))]\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:529:    :float64 (let [[neg e f] (float/fields (number/float-value x))]\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:733:  (let [h (float/float64->bits (number/float-value x))\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:36:(def integer-limits\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:46:  ([] (registry integer-limits))\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:51:       (prelude/register-integer-module limits)\\n    (let [v (get limits k)]\\n      (when-not (and (host/exact-integer? v)\\n                     (not (host/big-carrier? v))\\n                     (pos? v))\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"integer module needs explicit limits\\\"\\n                        {::reason :limits, ::limit k}))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn integer-module\\n  \\\"The `integer` module's exports under `limits`, export symbol to host\\n   function. `limits` must carry `::max-bits` and `::max-digits`.\\\"\\n  [limits]\\n  (check-limits! limits)\\n  (let [limits (select-keys limits [::max-bits ::max-digits])\\n        ;; chosen once: every fast result fits in 53 bits\\n        fast? (>= (::max-bits limits) 53)]\\n    (into {}\\n          (map (fn [[op arities f]]\\n                 (let [f (if fast? (get fast-kernels op f) f)]\\n                   [op (arity-checked op arities (partial f limits))])))\\n          exports)))\\n\\n\\n(def integer-profiles\\n  \\\"UCF 7.5.2 profiles for the `integer` module: every export `:pure`,\\n   declaring no effects and no host state.\\\"\\n  (into {}\\n        (map (fn [[op arities _]]\\n               [op (vm/primitive-profile op :pure arities #{} :none)]))\\n        exports))\\n\\n\\n(defn register-integer-module\\n  \\\"Return registry with the `integer` module installed under `limits`.\\n   Composition-only: the module is never in `yin.vm/primitives`.\\\"\\n  [registry limits]\\n  (module/register-host-module registry module-name (integer-module limits)\\n                               integer-profiles))\\n(ns yang.php.antlr.linked-harness\\n  \\\"The linked profile's composition for the PHP tests (P6, docs/design/\\n   yang.php.md section 11): the namespace that requires this is the\\n   publisher of `php`, so it publishes the module once per process into one\\n   in-memory content store, publishes a fixture's non-entry units beside it\\n   bottom-up, and serves them to the entry unit's run over a\\n   `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` composition under `:trusted` derivation.\\n\\n   A linked run builds the entry unit's task on one of the three vector VMs\\n   with a link pair, then runs the VM and serves the pair alternately until\\n   the VM stops blocking, as the REPL's drive does. The AST walker is not a\\n   backend here: its link of `php` is refused until L-b.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\\n    [yang.core.call :as call]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.lower :as lower]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.portable-harness :as portable]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.prelude :as prelude]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.publication :as publication]\\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\\n    [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\\n    [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\\n    [yin.vm.linker.interpreter :as link]\\n    [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\\n    [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\\n    [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\\n    [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as sr-linearize]\\n    [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\\n\\n\\n(defn registry\\n  \\\"The host registry a linked PHP composition registers: the `require`\\n   handlers, then `cell`, `data` and `integer` under the portable limits.\\\"\\n  []\\n  (portable/linked-registry))\\n\\n\\n(def published\\n  \\\"`yang.call` and `php` published once into their own content store.\\\"\\n  (delay\\n    (let [store (mem/create-content-mem)\\n          call-result (publish/publish-module! store (call/module-spec (registry)))\\n          result (publish/publish-module! store (prelude/module-spec (registry)\\n                                                                     (:address call-result)))]\\n      {:store store,\\n       :result result,\\n       :address (:address result),\\n       :call-address (:address call-result)})))\\n\\n\\n(defn publish-units!\\n  \\\"`units` (`[{:name path :packet p :strict-types b}]`) published into\\n   `php`'s own content store, where its pinned requirement already lives:\\n   `publication/publish-units!`'s answer.\\\"\\n  [units]\\n  (publication/publish-units! (:store @published) (registry) (:address @published) units))\\n\\n\\n(defn source\\n  \\\"The serving composition over the published store: `name-env` names `php`\\n   and each published module, derivation `:trusted`, the test being the\\n   publisher.\\\"\\n  [name-env]\\n  (link/composition {:content-store (:store @published),\\n                     :name-env (merge {'php (:address @published),\\n                                       'yang.call (:call-address @published)} name-env),\\n                     :derivation :trusted}))\\n\\n\\n(def backends\\n  \\\"Each vector backend's root task over a program AST and composition.\\\"\\n  {:semantic (fn [ast opts]\\n               (semantic/load-ast (semantic/create-vm opts)\\n                                  (vm/ast->datoms ast)\\n                                  vm/ast-contract)),\\n   :stack (fn [ast opts]\\n            (dvm/create-vm (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))\\n                           (assoc opts :contract vm/stack-contract))),\\n   :register (fn [ast opts]\\n               (rvm/create-vm (derive/register-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)))\\n                              (assoc opts :contract vm/register-contract)))})\\n\\n\\n(defn composition\\n  \\\"A task's composition over `modules` and the link `pair`.\\\"\\n  [modules pair]\\n  {:make-stream tu/make-stream,\\n   :capability-secret tu/secret,\\n   :secret-source (fn [origin] (str tu/secret \\\"/\\\" (name origin))),\\n   :primitives vm/primitives,\\n   :modules modules,\\n   :link-request (:requests pair),\\n   :link-response (:responses pair)})\\n\\n\\n(defn drive\\n  \\\"Run `task` and serve `pair` from `src` alternately until the task stops\\n   blocking, at most 80 rounds. Returns `[task pair]`.\\\"\\n  [task pair src]\\n  (loop [task (vm/run task)\\n         pair pair\\n         i 0]\\n    (if (and (vm/blocked? task) (< i 80))\\n      (let [pair (:pair (link/serve {:pair pair, :source src}))]\\n        (recur (vm/run task) pair (inc i)))\\n      [task pair])))\\n\\n\\n(defn run-linked\\n  \\\"The finished task of the linked program `ast` on `backend`, served from\\n   `src`. A refused link surfaces as the program's own diagnostics.\\\"\\n  [backend ast src]\\n  (let [pair (link/make-pair)\\n        task ((get backends backend) ast (composition (registry) pair))]\\n    (first (drive task pair src))))\\n\\n\\n(defn name-env\\n  \\\"The name environment naming every published module of `result`\\n   (`publish-units!`'s answer): module symbol to address.\\\"\\n  [result]\\n  (merge (into {} (map (fn [[path address]] [(lower/path-module-symbol path) address]))\\n               (:addresses result))\\n         (:bundles result)))\\n\\n\\n(defn run-fixture\\n  \\\"`{backend value}` for the multi-unit fixture `units` (`{path packet}`,\\n   the entry unit `main`, default `lower/entry-path`) on the three vector\\n 